+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a new service for larder, or weighs two good options that no record settles. It holds what the
+team favors. Written after the same argument ran a third time: Redis for sessions in 2023, Redis for Celery's queue
+in March 2025, then Elasticsearch for pantry item search in issue 70, each time settled by Postgres doing the job.
+
+## Tenets
+
+### A new service must do what Postgres cannot, with two volunteers able to restore it
+
+Larder runs one Postgres database and one web app, and the volunteers who run it are few. This rules out adding a
+service for a job Postgres already does, and a service only one volunteer can restore: in March 2025 the Redis
+instance holding the job queue evicted 300 receipt jobs one Saturday night, and only one volunteer could restore it.
+It does not rule out a hosted service nobody in the team has to run, such as the card gateway. In issue 70,
+Elasticsearch was proposed because searching the pantry for "rice" took four seconds and missed "Rice, brown";
+Postgres full-text search on the item name answered in 40 ms and found it. Applied in
+[keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+[run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
